dr. Tara Narula joins us at the table to  discuss Tara I think most people hadn't  even heard of this procedure until  yesterday so what is it exactly and how  do you discover it an embolization is a  procedure we've had since the 1970s and  basically the idea is to cut off the  blood supply to a lesion in order to  shrink it or to prevent bleeding and so  what we do is we take a catheter which  is a very tiny small tube almost  spaghetti sized it goes up through an  artery in the groin into the aorta and  then into one of the renal arteries the  artery that supplies blood to the kidney  you then find the smaller artery that's  supplying blood to that particular  lesion and through the catheter you  inject some sort of material or  particles a gel foam or coil that  essentially closes that blood vessel off  so no blood oxygen or nutrients can get  to the lesion it shrinks and essentially  that tissues I only planned in advance  those kind of procedures yes yes it was  interesting there are so few details but  one thing they did say was not benign so  what they're trying to say is not camp  they said it was benign exactly exactly  so not cancerous and there are many  different types of benign lesions we  have no idea which one she had the most  common one would be something called an  angio mile lipoma a collection of blood  vessel tissue as well as muscle cells  and fat cells can be found in women in  the middle age or arteriovenous  malformations or aneurysms  she'll be there for a week we're told is  that normal so typically for  embolization it's an outpatient  procedure you do go home the next day or  even the same day the fact that she's  staying for a week may be that they're  taking extra precautions it could be  that her lesion was particularly complex  or large or there can be complications  rarely something called post  embolization syndrome where you can get  fever nausea vomiting and pain or other  complications like infection bleeding in  the groin contrast problem with the IV  dye they use that affects the kidneys so  there are other complications that can  happen all right  dr. Narula thank you so much
 

